/*!
 * \brief Base-64 support
 *
 * It is common to find attached objects in character transmissions using Base-64 as the de facto
 * encoding standard. This library natively implements a bridge to be supported by octet streams.
 *
 * \note
 * **Example**:
 * The PEM (Privacy-Enhanced Mail) format, which is often used in cryptography to represent
 * DER-encoded objects in a character stream.
 *
 * In order to represent ASCII printable characters, a maximum of 6 bits are used from each byte to
 * represent one octet. This means that each octet needs more than one character to be represented.
 *
 * \note
 * **Rationale**:
 * Unlike binary or hexadecimal, this base is not trivial, yet it is universally adopted. It makes
 * sense to implement it.
 *
 * All types of character streams are tolerated through ber::b64_source_traits and
 * ber::b64_sink_traits.
 *
 * \addtogroup in-out-base64
 */

